"details": {"1": {
    "id":  1,
    "title":  "Damaged Flight Helmet",
    "subject":  "HISTORY",
    "grade":  "9",
    "description":  "A battle-scarred flight helmet from a cockpit â?? seven times bigger than the old placeholder models. In AR you can walk around it: the cracked visor, oxygen mask and ear-cups tell the story of early aviation safety. Tap each part to see how the design protected pilots.",
    "modelUrl":  "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/KhronosGroup/glTF-Sample-Assets@main/Models/DamagedHelmet/glTF-Binary/DamagedHelmet.glb",
    "markerId":  "ARU-HELMET-101",
    "audioUrl":  null,
    "videoUrl":  null,
    "status":  "PUBLISHED",
    "version":  1,
    "createdBy":  "Dev Arjun Mehta",
    "trackingTestReport":  null,
    "averageRating":  0.0,
    "parts":  [
                  {
                      "id":  1,
                      "partName":  "shell",
                      "label":  "Outer Shell",
                      "explanation":  "A hardened, crack-resistant shell is what stands between the pilot and debris. The \"damaged\" pitting on this example is real flight history - inspect it in AR and notice how the cracks branch instead of punching through, because the shell is meant to deform, not shatter.",
                      "audioUrl":  null
                  },
                  {
                      "id":  2,
                      "partName":  "visor",
                      "label":  "Cracked Visor",
                      "explanation":  "The visor is a laminated transparency: even when the outer layer crazes, inner layers keep the pilot\u0027s view. The visible crack pattern is a textbook case of spreading impact energy across many laminated sheets.",
                      "audioUrl":  null
                  },
                  {
                      "id":  3,
                      "partName":  "oxygen_mask",
                      "label":  "Oxygen Mask",
                      "explanation":  "At altitude there is barely enough oxygen to breathe, so the mask feeds the pilot from a pressure supply. Its seal has to stay airtight while the head moves and talks.",
                      "audioUrl":  null
                  },
                  {
                      "id":  4,
                      "partName":  "ear_cup_left",
                      "label":  "Left Ear Cup",
                      "explanation":  "Twin speakers and microphones live inside. Comms headsets solve a real problem: in a roaring cockpit you cannot hear speech, so the ear cup must isolate and amplify.",
                      "audioUrl":  null
                  },
                  {
                      "id":  5,
                      "partName":  "ear_cup_right",
                      "label":  "Right Ear Cup",
                      "explanation":  "Often the channel for the radio transmit microphone. Comparing left and right you can see how early headsets put different functions on each side.",
                      "audioUrl":  null
                  },
                  {
                      "id":  6,
                      "partName":  "chin_strap",
                      "label":  "Chin Strap",
                      "explanation":  "A helmet that flies off at the first jolt does not protect anyone. The strap is sized for a quick-release buckle - one pull frees the pilot if the aircraft is inverted or sinking.",
                      "audioUrl":  null
                  },
                  {
                      "id":  7,
                      "partName":  "comm_port",
                      "label":  "Comm Port",
                      "explanation":  "The cable connector plumbed directly to the aircraft\u0027s radio. It has to survive vibration and sweat for months without losing contact - a lesson in why aviation connectors are over-engineered.",
                      "audioUrl":  null
                  },
                  {
                      "id":  8,
                      "partName":  "liner",
                      "label":  "Impact Liner",
                      "explanation":  "Inside the hard shell sits a soft crushable liner that lengthens the time of any impact, cutting peak G-forces on the skull. Same principle as a car\u0027s crumple zone, scaled to a head.",
                      "audioUrl":  null
                  }
              ],
    "questions":  [
                      {
                          "id":  1,
                          "questionText":  "Why is the helmet\u0027s shell intentionally designed to deform on impact?",
